title Caravan ladder -- the printing two meters cannot see
prompt Take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, Standfast on reds first, one council-rota doc per lap, send each finished increment as its own signed round.
think Standfast first. The living REDS ledger holds five rows -- %96, %97, %98, %99, %100 -- and every one reads CLOSED with its proof named. The eight bare numbers a naive grep flags as open (%63, %67, %69, %72, %73, %80, %83, %93) are citations inside closed rows and folded archive ranges rather than living rows. No open agent-closable red stands, so the line is not stopped and constructive work may begin.
think Council rota by lap index. The index holds 1,474 rows, and 1474 mod 27 + 1 lands on item 17, `context/LEXICON.md`. Deep-read the pattern section (module - aspect - graduation), the core stack, the referential namespace, and the type-marks. The graduation rule -- an aspect becomes a module when its second consumer arrives from outside its host -- reads directly onto this lap: a third meter is not a new module, it is a third reading of one ladder, and it belongs beside its two kin rather than in a home of its own.
think The running thread is unambiguous. The newest log's recommend names the crux in a number: the printing staircase, three lines a rung across every rung holding it, invisible to the byte-identical meter for exactly the reason the orchestration spine was. Fold D wrote the seam a lift would use. The named shape of the answer is a meter beside the spine meter, then a lift into the harness -- so this lap is the meter, and the fold is its own round.
think Measure before designing. The copy scan reads `^(pub )?fn check_[a-z0-9_]*\(` and the spine scan reads one function name in `CARAVAN_SPINE_FN`. The printing family opens on `tell_`, so it falls outside both windows -- and confirming that by reading the two scans rather than assuming it is what makes this a ratchet rather than an erratum. Neither meter measured anything wrong; each named its own window in its own first sentence.
think The hand measurement, run before a line of the scan was written: 215 printing bodies over 9,317 lines across 42 of the 102 rung modules, 2,468 lines distinct, 6,849 carried. Seven and a half times the 897 the spine carried before fold D, and the largest carry standing on the ladder today.
think A second split matters more than the total, because it decides what the fold should be. Hashing whole bodies rather than lines shows 93 of the 215 standing byte-for-byte in a rung below, carrying 3,164 lines. That third of the carry lifts exactly the way a check body lifted under fold B -- the ladder has already done that lift twice and knows its cost. The rest is the staircase, and it wants fold D's `comptime` seam. Sizing the carry as one undifferentiated number would have hidden that a third of the answer is already written.
think The scan therefore reports three readings rather than one: PRINT_BODIES for the whole-body split, PRINT_LINES for the union line carry, and PRINT_NEIGHBOR for a rung-by-rung walk that asks a different question over the same extraction. Two readings that agree by construction would prove nothing (REDS %93), so the walk is kept genuinely independent and its gap from the union is explained rather than smoothed.
think The two readings land at 6,849 and 6,641 -- within three percent. The gap is honest and nameable: the union counts a line dropped by one rung and written again further up the size ordering, and the printing family runs in more than one chain (the apprise and reweigh branches beside the long correspondence arc), so ordering by size places a few neighbors that are not neighbors on the ladder. Stating what separates two readings is what makes their agreement worth something.
think The ceiling wants sizing rather than guessing. 6,849 stand and a new rung adds roughly 450, so 7,800 catches the second rung written before the fold lands rather than the tenth -- the same discipline the spine scan used at 1,100 against 897 before fold D brought it to 40.
think The counter must be proven rather than trusted. The two-rung corpus holds both shapes on purpose: the foot writes one three-line telling, the rung above writes that telling byte for byte plus a second that is the first plus one line of its own. Ten lines, five distinct, five carried; three bodies, one a whole copy of three lines; the neighbor walk reading 3. Every number countable by eye in the fixture, and the scan matched all of them on the first run.
obs A ratchet, never a red, and the distinction is load-bearing. The copy scan's own first sentences say BYTE-IDENTICAL in capitals and the spine scan names its single function in its second paragraph. Nothing claimed coverage it lacked, so this is a third window on one ladder rather than an erratum against either. REDS %97's lesson was a count that cannot see what it measures; both elder meters see exactly what they say they see.
obs The whole-body split is the round's most useful number. 3,164 of the 6,849 carried lines sit in 93 bodies that stand byte-for-byte in a rung below, which means a third of this carry has fold B's answer already written for it. Reading the parts of a measured number rather than its sum is the same move that found REDS %98, and it pays again here.
obs TAME's seventy-line ratchet, reported by the new scan at nine bodies past the bound with `refrain@tell_beckon_own:111` longest, is independently confirmed by `tame_style_check` naming the same bodies -- two tools agreeing on a count neither took from the other.
obs A fabricated stamp caught in my own prose before it landed. The scan and witness headers first read `20260820.221500`, ahead of the live clock at `20260820.221349` -- exactly REDS %96's class. Corrected against the clock rather than rationalized, and both files re-run GREEN after.
obs The lap adds no line to any rung and changes no behavior. It is measurement only, which is why the three meters can be run together and all three read GREEN unchanged beside each other.
